# Without Vite

The Vite plugin reads files, finds the names your modules export, serves modules that don't exist on disk and reloads pages as you edit. [`compile`](/docs/reference/compile) does none of that. It takes text and returns Svelte source, never touches the filesystem and runs in a browser, a worker or Node. Everything below is what a build tool, a playground or an editor that isn't Vite supplies to get the same output.

Import the compiler from `mdsvex/compile`, highlighting from `mdsvex/highlight` and math from `mdsvex/math`. None of them loads Vite or a Node builtin, and neither does `mdsvex/host`, which holds the pieces of the plugin a host needs beside the compiler.

::marks

## What a host supplies

Each feature, and what a host gives `compile` for it:

::::added
:::add[markdown, frontmatter, `metadata`](mark="---", tone=text)
nothing
:::

:::add[footnotes, smart punctuation, heading ids, `unwrap_images`](mark="[^]", tone=link)
the option
:::

:::add[highlighting](mark="```", tone=fence)
a highlighter from `mdsvex/highlight`, with its languages loaded up front, see [highlighting](/docs/reference/highlighting#highlighting-with-compile)
:::

:::add[math](mark="$", tone=code)
a renderer from `load_math` in `mdsvex/math`, see [math](/docs/reference/math#math-with-compile)
:::

:::add[images as imports](mark="![]", tone=link)
`import_images: true`, then a bundler that imports images, see [images as imports](/docs/reference/images)
:::

:::add[links](mark="[]()", tone=link)
`routes`, and `base` as a string or `{ import, from }`, see [links](/docs/reference/links#links)
:::

:::add[code from a file](mark="src=", tone=fence)
the text of each file in `sources`, or `read` and `resolve` with [`compile_async`](/docs/reference/compile#compile_async), see [code from a file](/docs/reference/highlighting#code-from-a-file)
:::

:::add[code that runs](mark="eval", tone=fence)
`snippets`, then serving each of `result.snippets` as a module, see [code that runs](/docs/reference/code-that-runs#running-with-compile)
:::

:::add[templates, components, directives](mark="as h2", tone=comp)
each module as its specifier and the names it exports, see below
:::

:::add[plugins](mark="p()", tone=other)
`plugins`, as for the Vite plugin, and [`compile_async`](/docs/reference/compile#compile_async) for one that [defers work](/docs/plugins/writing/deferred-work)
:::

:::add[plugins that load files](mark="read", tone=expr)
`read` and `resolve`, with `compile_async`, see [loading files](/docs/plugins/writing/deferred-work#loading-files)
:::

:::add[rebuilding when a file changes](mark="watch", tone=other)
watching `dependencies` on the result, see [below](/docs/guides/without-vite#files-and-plugins-that-wait)
:::

:::add[plugin data](mark="data", tone=expr)
nothing, it is `data` on the result, see [plugin data](/docs/plugins/writing/plugin-data)
:::

:::add[a template for one import](mark="?=", tone=link)
the `template` option of that compile, in place of `?template=`
:::
::::

## Finding the names a module exports

`compile` takes templates, components and directives as a specifier and the names that module exports (see [`compile`](/docs/reference/compile)), and writes the specifier into the output as given. The Vite plugin finds the names by reading each module's exports without running it.

::::plate[`read_exports`](mark="names", tone=expr)
`read_exports` from `mdsvex/host` does the same for you, with the TypeScript stripper you already have:

```js
import { read_exports } from 'mdsvex/host';
import ts from 'typescript';

const strip = (code) =>
	ts.transpileModule(code, { compilerOptions: { target: ts.ScriptTarget.ESNext } }).outputText;

const { names, stars, namespaces } = read_exports(source, 'svelte', strip);
```

:::notes
For a `.svelte` file it reads the `<script module>`. Without a stripper it reads the code as JavaScript. `stars` lists the `export * from` specifiers whose names it can't see, and `namespaces` the `export * as name from` ones, which is how a module [declares its directives](/docs/guides/directives#declaring-directives).
:::

:::edges
- `names` includes `default`. Leave it out for a template, whose default export is the template itself.
- For a [document used as a template](/docs/guides/templates#documents-as-templates), read the exports of its compiled `code` and leave out `metadata` as well.
:::
::::

## Virtual ids, `?metadata` and `?data`

::::plate[Virtual ids](mark="_ID", tone=expr)
The Vite plugin writes `mdsvex:components`, `mdsvex:template/<name>` and the other `mdsvex:*` ids in place of real paths and resolves them itself. `compile` writes whatever specifier you give it, so a host can use real paths and skip this.

:::notes
To match the plugin's output, `mdsvex/host` exports the prefixes (`COMPONENTS_ID`, `DIRECTIVES_ID`, `TEMPLATE_ID`, `TEMPLATE_DIRECTIVES_ID`, `PLUGIN_ID`, `PLUGIN_COMPONENTS_ID`), and `manifest_resolver(manifest)` returns the file an id and an imported name stand for.
:::
::::

::::plate[`?metadata` and `?data` modules](mark="?data", tone=link)
A [`?metadata`](/docs/reference/options#frontmatter) or [`?data`](/docs/plugins/writing/plugin-data) import loads a module made from the document.

:::notes
`metadata_module(source, parse)` builds the first from the text of the document, with your [frontmatter parser](/docs/guides/frontmatter#custom-frontmatter-parsers) or `undefined`, and `data_module(result.metadata, result.data)` the second from a compile of it.
:::
::::

## The editor manifest

::::plate[The editor manifest](mark=".json", tone=other)
The editor reads `node_modules/.mdsvex/manifest.json`, which the Vite plugin writes, or else `mdsvex.config.json`, which can't hold functions.

:::notes
A host can write the manifest itself. `queue_manifest(build, output)` writes it a little after the last change and only when it changed, `manifest_json` gives the exact text the plugin writes, and `MANIFEST_PATH` is where it goes.
:::
::::

## Vite only

These need a dev server or Vite's config, so they stay with the plugin:

::::added
:::add[`links.base: true`](mark="base", tone=link)
reads Vite's `base`. Give `compile` the base as a string, or as `{ import, from }` for a base known when the page renders.
:::

:::add[the checks on `?metadata` and `?data` imports](mark="?data", tone=link)
In dev the plugin serves a document only when Vite would serve the file, so a request can't read files outside the project.
:::

:::add[source maps back to markdown](mark=".map", tone=other)
`compile` with `sourcemap: true` maps its own output, and `mappings_to_v3` from `@mdsvex/render/sourcemap` turns that into a v3 map. Chaining it with the Svelte compiler's map is up to your bundler.
:::

:::add[`?template=` on an import](mark="?=", tone=link)
Use the `template` option of the compile.
:::

:::add[hot update](mark="hot", tone=other)
of documents, of the files a fence shows and of template and component modules. A host that rebuilds watches `dependencies` instead, see below.
:::
::::

## Files and plugins that wait

`compile` reads nothing and waits for nothing. There are two ways to give a document the files it shows and the work its plugins defer:

- **[`compile_async`](/docs/reference/compile#compile_async)** does it in one call. Pass `read` and `resolve` and it reads the files fences show with `src=` and what plugins load, then waits for deferred work. Use it for a build where the host can read files, in Node or a worker with a virtual filesystem.
- **[Compiling in steps](/docs/guides/without-vite#compiling-in-steps)** hands the waiting to you: `parse`, then `settle` for deferred work, then read each of `parsed.needs` yourself and `render`. Use it when files arrive another way, such as a fetch or an editor's open buffers, or to compile other documents while one waits. The playground compiles this way.

The result's `dependencies` are absolute paths: what plugins read or named, and, when `compile_async` read them, the files fences show and the module of the template. Compiling in steps, you know the files you read for `needs` yourself. To rebuild as the Vite plugin does in dev, watch those files, and when one changes call `session.invalidate([path])` on your `CompilerSession`, so what [`ctx.cached`](/docs/plugins/writing/deferred-work#loading-files) kept from it goes, then compile the documents that list it again. With `compile_async`, pass that session as its third argument.

## Compiling in steps

::::plate[Parse, settle, render](mark="settle", tone=expr)
A host that is not Vite, such as a playground or another bundler, compiles in steps so it can fetch what a document needs between them. This is what the Vite plugin does:

```js
import { CompilerSession, compile_inputs } from 'mdsvex/compile';

const session = new CompilerSession();
const inputs = compile_inputs(options); // the options compile takes
const parsed = session.parse(source, inputs);

// 1. what the plugins deferred, undefined when there is nothing to wait for
await session.settle(parsed);

// 2. what the document needs: the files its fences show with src=, and images
for (const need of parsed.needs ?? []) {
	if (need.type === 'source') sources[need.spec] = await read(need.spec);
}
inputs.sources = sources;

// 3. the render, with the same result compile gives
const { code, data } = session.render(parsed, inputs);
```

:::notes
- **`parse`** runs the parser and the plugins. The `Parsed` it returns holds the document, its `metadata`, the `data` its plugins wrote so far and `needs`.
- **`settle`** waits for deferred work and applies it, and returns `undefined` when there was none, so a document without any stays synchronous. A document whose plugins deferred work throws if it renders before.
- **Read `needs` after `settle`.** Deferred work and a `document` hook can add an image or a fence with `src=`, or change one, and `settle` makes `needs`, `snippets` and `math` again from the settled tree. Only what the inputs of the `parse` turned on is looked for.
- **`render`** gives what `compile` gives. A `Parsed` renders once.
- **The session compiles other documents while one waits.** Each `Parsed` holds its own document.
- **`parsed.dependencies`**, and `dependencies` on the result, are the absolute paths its plugins read or named, for the host to watch. When one changes, call `session.invalidate([path])` before compiling the documents that depend on it again, so what `ctx.cached` kept from it goes.
- **`read` and `resolve` options** give plugins `ctx.read` and `ctx.resolve`.
:::
::::
